  |title I'm being prescriptive out of fatigue — push back, refine, or even balk

  |quote
    (sorry, I'm being very prescriptive and imperative-- it's out of fatigue and just fast momentum-- please don't hesitate to push back, refine, or even balk if you feel the need ;-) )

  |occasion
    A parenthetical appended to his own message, late at night, four days before the
    Paper 3 deadline stretch. He diagnoses his own register mid-sprint and tells the
    agent what to discount it *for*.

  |about
    The generalizable move is the causal attribution, not the apology: he does not
    merely soften, he names the mechanism (fatigue, momentum) so the agent can weight
    the imperative correctly. An agent that knows *why* a brief is terse can read past
    the terseness; one that only knows the author is sorry cannot.

  |before-acting
    - You are inferring intent from the forcefulness of a request rather than its
      content.
    - You are about to treat time pressure in the *sender* as urgency in the *task*.

  |on-reading
    - A brief you received is unusually terse or imperative.
    - Joseph apologizes for his own tone.

  |see
    - #exm-not-ordering-you-a-recommendation
    - #exm-steward-caught-own-prescription

  |working-notes
    Also a specimen of the thing the estate's delegation file asks agents to do and
    which is hardest to do to oneself: second-passing the register you actually wrote
    in.

    Located with `memorata3-search` and carved from the primary by anchored span
    extraction (never retyped); verified back against the primary byte-for-byte after
    emission.
